 The updater uses saved network settings; CLI `-c` overrides do not reach it.
 
+### Cooperative upgrade checkpoints
+
+Before an explicit package update replaces a running daemon, it holds new work
+and publishes a retained upgrade notice to every loaded conversation, including
+idle conversations and worker conversations. Every attached tab receives its
+conversation's notice. Active turns are steered to pause safely, pause any native
+goal, and return a structured handoff. The updater writes that handoff atomically
+and syncs the file and containing directory under
+`CODEX_HOME/daemon-upgrades/upgrade-*/THREAD_ID.json`; it requires no workspace
+write permission or MCP setup. Idle conversations receive an explicitly labeled
+snapshot without starting a model turn. `state.json` records preparation progress
+and the handoff locations.
+
+Replacement waits up to 120 seconds for valid operation-bound handoffs, idle
+turns and preserved, inactive goals. A refusal, timeout or failed validation
+returns an `Upgrade held` error with the preparation path. The old daemon stays
+running and selected; new work is admitted again when preparation ends. Goals
+already paused remain paused. Inspect pending obligations before resuming work
+or retrying an update. Updating or reconnecting never renews goal limits.
+
+### Recovery after replacement
+
+Recovery-capable daemons seal original work before replacement. After a matching
+replacement answers the native control handshake, the updater and managed daemon
+startup coordinate through private operation/thread receipts in the same
+`daemon-upgrades` directory. Previously active work receives its durable handoff
+as internal model context; previously paused or idle work stays stopped. The
+thread, original root turn, permission policy, goal identity, token ceiling and
+consumed allowance are retained. No controller enrollment or MCP setup is needed.
+
+A later user or governor goal mutation, including paused-to-paused, cancels that
+conversation's automatic recovery. Changed permissions, executor, resource
+authority, an external deadline requiring live qualification, or a different
+replacement executable also hold recovery. Native notices explain resumed or
+held work. An accepted recovery is recorded by its turn ID; response loss does
+not authorize another turn. On another process restart, unfinished accepted work
+can continue under that same ID. Unknown delivery remains held rather than
+being inferred from absent history.
+
+`state.json` distinguishes `installing`, `recovering`, `recovered`,
+`recoveryHeld`, and first-adoption `installedPaused`. Installation success and
+recovery success are separate. Inspect the per-thread `*.recovery.json` receipt
+and retained handoff when recovery is held or unconfirmed. Existing records are
+preserved for diagnosis and rollback. A current daemon with checkpoint support
+but no recovery sealing may be upgraded, but this first adoption explicitly
+leaves its work paused. Subsequent upgrades can recover sealed work.
+
+Scheduled checks defer while any conversations are loaded, avoiding unsolicited
+pauses. Use an explicit update to request checkpoints. A running older daemon
+without `daemon/upgrade/prepare` cannot be upgraded cooperatively: save work and
+stop it once before adopting this version. The updater refuses an unsafe
+fallback. Explicit `stop` and `restart` retain their separate shutdown semantics.
+
+To select a retained complete package for rollback, invoke that package's
+`bin/codex app-server daemon update --from-cli --yes`. Preparation is enforced by
+the CLI performing the update, so prefer a checkpoint-capable CLI. When an older
+rollback CLI is required, verify all work is paused first. Do not overwrite the
+current executable in place. A successful checkpoint is separate from a
+successful replacement start; retain the previous package and preparation files
+when diagnosing a failed start.
+
+An external build-channel launcher must register the same qualified package after
+a successful selection, and restore its registration on rollback. This native
+updater does not modify downstream launcher registries. A mismatched registration
+should hold a new launch rather than silently start a different daemon.
+
 For all managed app-server shutdowns, including explicit stop and restart and
 updater-triggered restarts, `shutdownGraceSeconds` defaults to 60 and accepts
 an integer from 0 through 300. Zero forces shutdown immediately after requesting
